<ArticleLayout title="How Can I Better Track My Cortisol? Tests vs Wearables" description="Learn how to track cortisol with appropriate testing, and how a sleep, stress and wearable log can add context without measuring hormone levels." published="2026-09-16" updated="2026-09-16">
<p><strong>To track actual cortisol, you need an appropriate hormone test and a plan for interpreting it.</strong> To track everyday stress and recovery, record sleep, perceived stress and available wearable readings. These are different activities: a journal or an Apple Watch app cannot tell you your cortisol concentration.</p>
<p>This guide is published by the Cortisol+ team. We make a wearable wellness app, not a cortisol test. It is educational information, not a personalized testing recommendation.</p>
<h2>How can I better track my cortisol?</h2>
<p>Start by deciding what you want to learn. If you have a medical concern or an abnormal test result, ask your clinician which test, collection time and follow-up make sense. If you want to understand your daily routine, use a consistent observation log. Keep laboratory results separate from app scores, and avoid interpreting a change in sleep or HRV as proof that a hormone changed.</p>
