<p><a href="https://medlineplus.gov/lab-tests/cortisol-test/">MedlinePlus explains the types of cortisol tests</a> and why collection timing matters. Our <a href="/cortisol/test/">at-home cortisol tracking guide</a> explains the questions to ask before ordering or collecting a sample.</p>
<h2>A practical daily tracking workflow</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Record the date and sleep window.</strong> Note bedtime, wake time and interruptions in your own words.</li><li><strong>Add how you felt.</strong> Use a consistent personal description or scale for perceived stress and energy. This is a self-report, not a diagnosis.</li><li><strong>Record relevant context.</strong> Note changes in exercise, caffeine timing, alcohol, illness or routine. Do not change medication to improve a graph.</li><li><strong>Check available wearable samples.</strong> Confirm the source, units and timestamps. Missing data is not a low reading.</li><li><strong>Review patterns without assigning a cause.</strong> “These changed together” is a useful observation to investigate; it is not proof of a cortisol change.</li></ol>
<p>Use the <a href="/resources/stress-sleep-journal/">free seven-day stress and sleep journal</a> as a starting format. Seven days is an organizational choice, not a validated cortisol-testing schedule.</p>
<h2>What should I record alongside a cortisol test?</h2>
<p>Keep the laboratory report, units, reference range, sample type and collection date/time together. Tell the ordering clinician about medicines and relevant changes in your routine. Follow the collection instructions you were given, rather than a generic online schedule. Do not compare a saliva result directly with a blood result or assume results from different laboratories use identical ranges.</p>
<h2>Can I track cortisol without a test?</h2>
<p>You can track stress-related observations without a test, but you cannot confirm your cortisol level from them. <a href="/stress/apple-watch/">Apple Watch records signals such as heart rate, HRV and sleep</a>; it does not directly measure cortisol. An app can interpret those inputs, but that interpretation needs its own evidence and limitations.</p>
<h2>Where Cortisol+ fits</h2>
<p>Cortisol+ helps you review available wearable signals and the factors behind its wellness score. Read the <a href="/how-it-works/">methodology</a>, inspect the contributing records, and compare similar periods. Clinical accuracy against laboratory cortisol testing has not been established. The app cannot decide whether testing is needed or confirm that a habit lowered cortisol.</p>
